# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the concurrency suite.
#
# These suppress reports whose racing accesses are inside Kryoptic, not
# inside this provider. That is not a convenience: without them the real
# signal is unreadable, and a suppression file that hid our own races would
# defeat the entire point of running TSan.
#
# Why Kryoptic's reports are false positives, established rather than
# assumed:
#
#   - Kryoptic is linked as a prebuilt .so and is NOT instrumented, so TSan
#     sees its memory accesses but not its synchronisation.
#   - It guards its global state with `state: RwLock<State>` (src/lib.rs).
#     Rust's std::sync primitives on Linux are futex-based rather than
#     pthread-based, and TSan's happens-before edges come from intercepting
#     pthread calls. A futex it cannot see is synchronisation it cannot
#     credit, so every access under that RwLock looks unsynchronised.
#   - The provider passes CKF_OS_LOCKING_OK to C_Initialize
#     (src/pkcs11/p11_context.c), which is exactly what PKCS#11 v2.40 §5.4
#     requires of an application that will call the library from several
#     threads. Having declared that, the provider is entitled to rely on
#     the module's own locking, and Kryoptic provides it.
#
# The entries are named by the racing function, not by the library as a
# whole. `called_from_lib` would have been shorter and would also have
# suppressed races in OUR code that merely happen to occur on a stack
# passing through Kryoptic — which is the class of bug this suite exists
# to find.
#
# If a report appears that these do not cover, read it before adding to
# this file. The question is always which frame performs the racing
# ACCESS, not which frames appear in the stack.

race:kryoptic_lib::
race:hashbrown::
race:^rust_

# libsqlite3 — Kryoptic's storage backend, also linked uninstrumented.
#
# The racing access is sqlite3DbStrNDup on a block sqlite3 itself
# allocated; the provider's frames appear only as the caller. Whether
# Kryoptic shares one sqlite3 connection across threads without a mutex is
# a question about Kryoptic's storage layer, not about this provider, and
# nothing in this repository can fix it.
#
# Worth knowing if a future backend is evaluated: it is the token's job to
# be thread-safe once the application has passed CKF_OS_LOCKING_OK, which
# this provider does.
race:sqlite3
